+### Pagination classes
+
+The pagination key ships whole: the strip, its page links, the hover, focus, current, and
+unavailable states, the collapsed borders between adjacent pages, the rounded ends, and the small
+and large size classes. The component partial loads after the vertical rule in the components layer.
+
+Every value the family paints is Bootstrap 5.3.8's own. A length reads the Veneer scale token that
+already resolves to it, so the strip answers to `--vn-factor-density` for its padding and to
+`--vn-factor-radius` for its rounded ends, the way the Button family does. A color reads the
+compatibility variable Bootstrap itself names, so the resting page takes the body surface, the link
+color, and the border color, and the hover, focus, and unavailable states take the tertiary and
+secondary surfaces. The current page is the exception: Bootstrap paints it with the palette blue
+rather than with a variable, so the class reads `--vn-palette-blue` and `--vn-palette-white-base`,
+which carry that exact pair. The current page therefore holds one fill in both color modes while the
+pages around it retune.
+
+The focus ring is the recorded quarter-rem shadow at a quarter of the palette blue, written as a mix
+over that token because a partial declares no literal color. It replaces the native outline rather
+than sitting beside it, which is why the rule clears the outline at the same site.
+
+Stacking is what keeps each state's chrome whole. Adjacent pages share one border, because every page
+after the first pulls itself one border width towards the page before it. A hovered page lifts to `2`
+so its own border paints over that shared edge; a focused page and the current page lift to `3`, so
+the focus ring and the current fill are never cut by the page beside them. An unavailable page lifts
+to nothing and refuses the pointer outright.
+
+The state classes read the same on a page link and on its item: `.page-link.active` and
+`.active > .page-link` carry one treatment, and so do `.page-link.disabled` and
+`.disabled > .page-link`. A template can carry either class wherever its own markup puts it.
